 When physical state is explicitly recorded in an immutable ledger, the model reliably anchors its reasoning to this authoritative source. Across 12 judgments (six pairs with reversed answer-option order) evaluating IA1 under both minimal summary and extended 12-message history contexts, the model achieved 100\% accuracy in identifying unsatisfied scientific requirements across all state queries and reversed option orders. The delivery DAG thus provides an immutable coordinate system for subsequent reasoning: demarcating verified entities, pinpointing existing deficits, and determining whether to pursue additional calculations or submit partial deliverables reflecting physical reality.
 
-\subsection{Workflow guidance, limitations, and outlook}
 
-Guided achieved the strongest overall benchmark performance, although it did not clearly separate from the other non-Standard configurations on Easy tasks. Its advantage became more apparent as tasks required sustained pathway exploration, evidence assembly, and recovery from unsuccessful calculations. This pattern suggests that the value of Cloudra's workflow guidance depends on the research demands: basic tasks can often be handled through several routes, whereas more demanding tasks benefit from keeping geometric operations, computational evidence, and completion decisions connected throughout the exploration.
-
-Record provides an informative contrast. It retains access to the tools and diagnostic capabilities available to Guided, but removes mandatory scientific acceptance. Nevertheless, its performance fell behind Guided on Normal and Hard tasks. In the observed trajectories, the model did not consistently invoke the available capabilities or follow the recommended workflow, instead choosing ad hoc workarounds. Tool availability therefore does not ensure that the model will use those tools to build a complete scientific argument. The comparison supports coupling useful capabilities to explicit research requirements and feedback that directs the model back to unresolved evidence.
-
-Brief-errors offers a related lesson. Compressing diagnostics made some trajectories more prone to repeated computational attempts without resolving the underlying defect, as illustrated by the HA1 endpoint-linkage case above. With less specific guidance to return to the intended workflow, retries could accumulate substantial computational expenditure while the scientific gap persisted. Its lower performance than Guided is consistent with the importance of actionable diagnostics: feedback should identify both the failed requirement and the research action needed to address it. Together, Record and Brief-errors support Cloudra's design philosophy of connecting chemical intent to deterministic operations and using persistent evidence requirements to guide subsequent research.
-
-Standard had the shortest observed runtime, but its speed did not translate into comparable benchmark scores. Incomplete delivery was a pronounced and recurring problem in Standard: it frequently failed to produce delivery graphs during the formal benchmark runs. We reconstructed the missing delivery graphs during offline organization using only records and computational artifacts already present in the corresponding runs. This reconstruction added no calculations or new scientific conclusions. Even after the missing graphs were reconstructed, the scores remained low, indicating that the missing delivery format alone did not explain the performance gap. The contrast also shows why elapsed runtime should be interpreted alongside the amount of scientifically supported work delivered.
-
-The benchmark has resource-related limitations. Limited computational resources led us to run many sandboxes concurrently, and queueing for CPU resources may have delayed individual calculations. Such delays could reduce the calculations and evidence completed within a run's available budget and thereby affect its score. The present results consequently reflect both research behavior and the shared computational environment; CPU queueing remains a possible contributor to score differences rather than an established explanation for any particular configuration. Separating queueing time from active calculation time would help clarify this contribution in subsequent evaluations.
-
-Cloudra alleviates the difficulties of direct geometric reasoning and manipulation by language models, and its conformational sampling has improved substantially over earlier versions. Highly flexible systems nevertheless remain challenging. As conformational diversity grows, identifying relevant reactant arrangements, intermediates, and pathways can require substantially more sampling and quantum-chemical evaluation. The present system cannot yet be expected to handle much more complex, highly flexible systems autonomously within a small computational budget. More efficient conformational exploration and better allocation of calculations among competing candidates are therefore important directions for further development.
-
-Our longer-term goal extends beyond small-molecule mechanism calculations to a broader range of computational chemistry. The current support for multiple computational engines, shared execution infrastructure, and persistent scientific records already provides a basis for that expansion. Extending coverage will require appropriate representations, methods, and validation criteria for each new class of problem. We aim for Cloudra to become a powerful research tool for computational chemists and a computational specialist for experimental teams. Cloudra will be released as open-source software under the GNU Affero General Public License (AGPL), supporting inspection, reuse, and community development of the system.
